IO - Computah OS library
(generated from docs/API.md)

Streams: files, the terminal and pipes. io.stdin,
io.stdout and io.stderr belong to the running
process, so the shell can redirect them per
program. print writes to io.stdout.

io.open(path[, mode]) -> stream

Opens a file as a buffered stream. mode: "r"
(default), "w" (truncate), "a" (append),
optionally with "b" (no difference: all files are
bytes). Returns nil, reason if it can't be opened.

  local f = assert(io.open("/home/notes.txt", "w"))
  f:write("line 1\n", 42, "\n")
  f:close()
  for line in io.lines("/home/notes.txt") do print(line) end

io.lines([path[, format...]]) -> iterator

Iterates over the lines of a file (closing it at
the end), or of io.input() without a path. Formats
as for stream:read.

io.read(format...) -> ...

io.input():read(...). From the terminal this reads
a line typed by the user.

io.write(value...) -> stream

io.output():write(...).

io.input([file]) -> stream

The current process's standard input; with a
stream or a path, replaces it first.

io.output([file]) -> stream

The current process's standard output; with a
stream or a path (opened for writing), replaces it
first.

io.error([file]) -> stream

The current process's standard error stream; with
a stream, replaces it first. On the terminal it
prints in red.

io.close([stream])

Closes a stream (default: the standard output).

io.type(value) -> string

"file" for an open stream, "closed file" for a
closed one, otherwise nil.

io.popen(command[, mode]) -> stream

Runs a shell command in the background and
connects to it. Mode "r" (default): returns a
stream reading what the command writes. Mode "w":
returns a stream whose data becomes the command's
input; close it to finish.

  local p = io.popen("ls /bin")
  for line in p:lines() do print("program: " .. line) end

io.tmpfile() -> stream

Creates a new empty file in /tmp and returns it
open for writing.

io.pipe() -> writer, reader

A connected pair of streams: what is written to
writer can be read from reader (reads wait for
data; they end when the writer is closed). Used by
the shell for a | b.

io.stream(backend) -> stream

Makes a buffered stream from a table with any of
readChunk(self) -> string|nil, writeChunk(self,
data), seekTo(self, whence, offset) and
closeIt(self). For writing your own stream types.

io.isTerminal(stream) -> boolean

Whether a stream is the terminal (programs format
output differently for files and pipes, like ls).

Stream methods

Method - Description
stream:read(format...) - "l" a line without the
    newline (default), "L" with it, "n" a number,
    "a" everything left, or a number of bytes.
    Returns nil at the end.
stream:lines([format]) - Iterator over reads.
stream:write(value...) - Writes strings and
    numbers; returns the stream, or nil, reason.
stream:seek([whence[, offset]]) - "set", "cur"
    (default) or "end"; returns the new position.
    Files only.
stream:close() - Closes it.
stream:flush(), stream:setvbuf() - No-ops, for
    compatibility.
